            <ArticleTitle>The Fourth Amendment and random drug testing of people with chronic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>42-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/15360288.2010.549549</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>It is common for physicians who prescribe opioids for chronic pain to drug test their patients. This practice may soon be mandated by the State of Washington as a result of passage of their new law ESHB 2876. Random drug testing of people simply because they seek treatment for chronic pain arguably constitutes a suspicionless and warrantless search that violates both the Fourth and Fourteenth Amendments. Issues discussed include consent, circumstantial coercion, and &quot;special needs&quot; searches.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
